n// becomes the first — and in a list whose positions are its meaning that turns a\n// bad input into a wrong answer instead of a loud one. `listDir` refuses for the\n// same reason and the reason is stronger here.\n//\n// THE ERRNO STAYS INSIDE. `std:fs` exposes `__fs_errno__`'s ambient cell in its\n// contract because its callers branch on the code; here no caller can, so the\n// empty-plus-errno protocol is read once inside the loop and never surfaces.\n// This module has no ambient state in its API and no order-dependent call.\n//\n// ONE `__trap__`, AND IT IS NOT A FAILURE MODE. `std:fs` opens by saying its\n// count is zero; this module's is one, so it is named here rather than found.\n// It fires only if the floor refuses an index BELOW the count it just reported —\n// a host contradicting itself, which is a bug and not a condition any caller can\n// cause or handle. Nothing a user can type reaches it.\n//\n// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO.\n//\n// NO FLAG PARSING — not \"not yet here\", but a different module. Every one of\n// short vs long, clustered `-abc`, `--name=value` vs `--name value`, `--` as\n// end-of-options, a repeated flag (last wins, or accumulate), `-1` as a value\n// rather than a flag, and typed/required/defaulted values is a policy decision,\n// and a parser that decides half of them silently disagrees with whatever the\n// caller assumed about the other half. A half-parser is worse than none. It also\n// wants a map to return its results into and `std:map` does not exist yet. None\n// of it needs a new floor intrinsic — a parser is a pure `string[] => …` over\n// what this returns — so deferring costs nothing and buys the whole design.\n//\n// NO BYTES ACCESSOR. `std:fs` offers `readFile` beside `readTextFile` because a\n// PNG must survive the round trip; the argv equivalent would be a non-UTF-8 path\n// argument, and there is nowhere to put one: every `std:fs` entry point takes\n// `path: string`, so the bytes could not be used to open the file they name.\n// When `std:fs` grows a bytes-taking path — or when process spawn arrives — a\n// `programArgBytes(i): u8[]` becomes admissible; today it has no consumer.\n//\n// NO LOSSY VARIANT. `decodeUtf8Lossy` is `std:utf8`'s deliberate opt-out for a\n// caller who would rather render mojibake than stop, and no argv consumer in\n// this tree wants it: a mangled argument is a wrong filename or a wrong number,\n// not a log line to squint at. It becomes admissible on the same day the bytes\n// accessor does, and out of the same export.\n//\n// NO ENVIRONMENT, NO EXIT CODE, NO PROGRAM NAME, NO STDIN. Each is a separate\n// floor intrinsic that does not exist (`env` + `exit` are item 5 of the same\n// ROADMAP list and a process exit code sits inside item 4), and a std wrapper\n// for a syscall VL cannot make is a name that fails at emit.\n//\n// ONE HOST WART, RECORDED BECAUSE IT IS INVISIBLE FROM IN HERE. `vl run p.vl -v x`\n// delivers ONE argument, `x`: the host's own flag loop matches `--compiler`,\n// `-e` and `--`, routes every other non-dash token into the vector, and falls\n// through anything else beginning with `-` to a catch-all that discards it with\n// no diagnostic (measured). `vl run p.vl -- -v x` delivers both. Nothing in this\n// module can tell the two apart — a dropped argument and an un-passed one are\n// the same absence — so a VL program taking dash-led arguments must be invoked\n// after `--`, and `tests/vl_std_args_test.ts` pins the behaviour so a change to\n// it is noticed rather than silently reshaping what programs see. Filed as\n// issue #1818; a fix there makes this paragraph stale rather than wrong, and the\n// pinning test is what will say so.\n\nimport { Utf8Error, decodeUtf8 } from \"std:utf8\"\n\n// RE-EXPORTED so a caller of the fallible export can spell its own error arm from\n// the module it imported: `import { Utf8Error, programArgs } from \"std:args\"`.\n// Without this the caller needs a second import from `std:utf8` to